aquinas-third-way.md:1: argue/readings: S3 is read 2 ways: aquinas-third-way.logic.r1 has "all_contingent → each_sometime_not"; aquinas-third-way.logic.r1.2 and aquinas-third-way.logic.r1.3 have "each_sometime_not"; they differ when nothing is true, which the first allows
aquinas-third-way.md:1: argue/answered: S3: answered no, which keeps aquinas-third-way.logic.r1.2 and aquinas-third-way.logic.r1.3, to: Suppose "everything is possible not to be" and "each thing that is possible not to be at some time is not" are false. Is S3, "But it is impossible for these always to exist, for that which is possible not to be at some time is not.", true then? [in 1 of 3 runs]
aquinas-third-way.md:1: argue/readings: S5 is read 2 ways: aquinas-third-way.logic.r1 and aquinas-third-way.logic.r1.3 have "only_from_existing"; aquinas-third-way.logic.r1.2 has "only_from_existing" and "only_from_existing → (once_nothing → now_nothing)"; they differ when only "at one time there was nothing in existence" and "that which does not exist only begins to exist by something already existing" are true, which the first allows
aquinas-third-way.md:1: argue/answered: S5: answered no, which keeps aquinas-third-way.logic.r1.2, to: Suppose "at one time there was nothing in existence" and "that which does not exist only begins to exist by something already existing" are true and "even now nothing would be in existence" is false. Is S5, "Now if this were true, even now there would be nothing in existence, because that which does not exist only begins to exist by something already existing.", true then?
aquinas-third-way.md:1: argue/readings: S6 is read 2 ways: aquinas-third-way.logic.r1 has "once_nothing ∧ only_from_existing → now_nothing" and "¬now_nothing"; aquinas-third-way.logic.r1.2 and aquinas-third-way.logic.r1.3 have "¬now_nothing"; they differ when only "at one time there was nothing in existence" and "that which does not exist only begins to exist by something already existing" are true, which the second allows
aquinas-third-way.md:1: argue/answered: S6: answered no, which keeps aquinas-third-way.logic.r1, to: Suppose "at one time there was nothing in existence" and "that which does not exist only begins to exist by something already existing" are true and "even now nothing would be in existence" is false. Is S6, "Therefore, if at one time nothing was in existence, it would have been impossible for anything to have begun to exist; and thus even now nothing would be in existence--which is absurd.", true then?
aquinas-third-way.md:1: argue/readings: S9 is read 2 ways: aquinas-third-way.logic.r1 has "¬regress"; aquinas-third-way.logic.r1.2 and aquinas-third-way.logic.r1.3 have "¬◇regress"; they differ when only "possibly, there is an infinite series of necessary things which have their necessity caused by another" is true, which the first allows
aquinas-third-way.md:1: argue/modal-ambiguous: S9 is read aquinas-third-way.logic.r1 with no modality; aquinas-third-way.logic.r1.2 and aquinas-third-way.logic.r1.3 as what is necessary or possible: the text does not say which it means [in 2 of 3 runs]
aquinas-third-way.md:1: argue/answered: S9: answered no, which keeps aquinas-third-way.logic.r1.2 and aquinas-third-way.logic.r1.3, to: Suppose "possibly, there is an infinite series of necessary things which have their necessity caused by another" and "possibly, there is an infinite series of necessary things which have their necessity caused by another" are true and "there is an infinite series of necessary things which have their necessity caused by another" is false. Is S9, "Now it is impossible to go on to infinity in necessary things which have their necessity caused by another, as has been already proved in regard to efficient causes.", true then? [in 1 of 3 runs]
aquinas-third-way.logic.r1: argue/readings: what the text leaves unstated is read 3 ways: aquinas-third-way.logic.r1 has "¬all_contingent → necessary_exists" and "all_caused → regress"; aquinas-third-way.logic.r1.2 has "each_sometime_not → (all_contingent → ◇once_nothing)" and "¬all_contingent → necessary_exists" and "necessary_exists ∧ all_caused → regress"; they differ when only "everything is possible not to be", "each thing that is possible not to be at some time is not", "there exists something the existence of which is necessary", "every necessary thing has its necessity caused by another" and "there is an infinite series of necessary things which have their necessity caused by another" are true, which the first allows; aquinas-third-way.logic.r1.3 has "all_contingent ∧ each_sometime_not → ◇once_nothing" and "only_from_existing → (once_nothing → now_nothing)" and "¬all_contingent → necessary_exists" and "all_caused → regress"
aquinas-third-way.logic.r1: argue/readings: U1: aquinas-third-way.logic.r1 and aquinas-third-way.logic.r1.3 take for granted "if every necessary thing has its necessity caused by another, then there is an infinite series of necessary things which have their necessity caused by another"; aquinas-third-way.logic.r1.2 does not
aquinas-third-way.logic.r1: argue/answered: U1: answered yes, which keeps aquinas-third-way.logic.r1 and aquinas-third-way.logic.r1.3, to: Does the text's argument take this for granted, whether or not it says so: "if every necessary thing has its necessity caused by another, then there is an infinite series of necessary things which have their necessity caused by another"? Answer yes if the author would accept it as part of the argument.
aquinas-third-way.logic.r1: argue/readings: U2: aquinas-third-way.logic.r1.2 and aquinas-third-way.logic.r1.3 take for granted "if each thing that is possible not to be at some time is not, then (if everything is possible not to be, then possibly, at one time there was nothing in existence)"; aquinas-third-way.logic.r1 does not [in 1 of 3 runs]
aquinas-third-way.logic.r1: argue/answered: U2: answered yes, which keeps aquinas-third-way.logic.r1.2 and aquinas-third-way.logic.r1.3, to: Does the text's argument take this for granted, whether or not it says so: "if each thing that is possible not to be at some time is not, then (if everything is possible not to be, then possibly, at one time there was nothing in existence)"? Answer yes if the author would accept it as part of the argument. [in 1 of 3 runs]
